---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/ias/ias21.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        {
          id: "s1",
          label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage",
        },
        {
          id: "s2",
          label: "Scope & Applicability",
        },
        {
          id: "s3",
          label: "Determining Functional Currency",
        },
        {
          id: "s4",
          label: "Foreign Currency Transactions",
        },
        {
          id: "s5",
          label: "Monetary vs Non-Monetary Items",
        },
        {
          id: "s6",
          label: "Translating to Presentation Currency",
        },
        {
          id: "s7",
          label: "Disposal of a Foreign Operation",
        },
        {
          id: "s8",
          label: "Net Investment in a Foreign Operation",
        },
        {
          id: "s9",
          label: "Disclosure Requirements",
        },
        {
          id: "s10",
          label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates",
        },
        {
          id: "s11",
          label: "IFRS vs US GAAP",
        },
        {
          id: "s12",
          label: "Statement Mapping",
        },
        {
          id: "s13",
          label: "Related Standards",
        },
        {
          id: "s14",
          label: "Glossary",
        },
        {
          id: "history",
          label: "History & Development",
        },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Why translation is a measurement problem, not just arithmetic</div>
        <div class="principle">The Conceptual Framework requires assets, liabilities, income, and expenses to be measured faithfully in the CURRENCY that best reflects the economic substance of an entity's transactions. IAS 21 exists to determine that currency (functional currency) and to ensure foreign currency amounts are translated in a way that preserves faithful representation.</div>
        <div class="elabel">HOW IAS 21 MAPS TO THE FRAMEWORK</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span><strong style="font-weight:600">Faithful representation</strong> — the functional currency concept ensures an entity's PRIMARY economic environment drives its measurement currency, not simply the currency it happens to report in</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:600">Relevance</strong> — recognising FX gains/losses on monetary items as they arise (rather than deferring them) reflects genuine changes in economic resources/obligations</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:600">Comparability</strong> — a consistent, principles-based translation methodology allows group financial statements to meaningfully combine operations across multiple currencies</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">The functional currency determination is deliberately a matter of FACT, not a free accounting policy choice — it is determined by the primary economic environment the entity operates in, which the Framework treats as an objective, verifiable matter.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Measurement principles applied by IAS 21</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 21's rules split cleanly by WHETHER an item's underlying value is fixed in currency terms (monetary) or subject to a separate measurement basis (non-monetary) — directly following the Framework's asset/liability characteristics.</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Item type</span><span>Exchange rate used</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Monetary items</span><span>Closing rate at EVERY reporting date</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Non-monetary items at historical cost</span><span>Rate at the date of the original transaction</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Non-monetary items at fair value</span><span>Rate when fair value was determined</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info" style="margin-bottom:0;">ℹ️ <strong style="font-weight:600">Qualitative characteristics in action:</strong> retranslating monetary items at EVERY reporting date reflects <strong style="font-weight:600">relevance</strong> — a foreign currency debt's real economic burden genuinely changes as exchange rates move, and faithful representation requires this to be shown.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What IAS 21 covers</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 21 applies to accounting for transactions and balances in foreign currencies, EXCEPT those derivative transactions and balances within IFRS 9's scope; translating results and financial position of foreign operations included in the entity's financial statements; and translating results/financial position into a presentation currency.</div>
        <div class="elabel">TWO DISTINCT PROBLEMS IAS 21 SOLVES</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;margin-bottom:5px;">(1) How does an individual entity account for TRANSACTIONS denominated in a currency OTHER than its functional currency?</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">(2) How does a group TRANSLATE the results of a foreign operation into the presentation currency used for consolidated financial statements?</div>
        <div class="note">These are genuinely separate questions with DIFFERENT mechanics (Sections 4–5 for the first, Section 6 for the second) — a common source of confusion.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Functional currency</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The currency of the primary economic environment in which the entity operates.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Presentation currency</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The currency in which the financial statements are presented — may DIFFER from the functional currency (an entity is free to present in any currency).</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Foreign currency</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">A currency OTHER than the functional currency of the entity.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Foreign operation</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">An entity that is a subsidiary, associate, joint arrangement, or branch of the reporting entity, the activities of which are based/conducted in a country/currency OTHER than those of the reporting entity.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;">
          <div class="glossary-term">Closing rate</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The spot exchange rate at the end of the reporting period.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Determining Functional Currency</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Primary and secondary indicators</div>
        <div class="principle">Functional currency is determined based on economic substance, not choice. IAS 21 provides a hierarchy of indicators; primary indicators usually determine the answer, with secondary indicators as tie-breakers.</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">PRIMARY INDICATORS</div>
            <div class="check-list">
              <span>The currency that mainly INFLUENCES sales prices for goods/services (often the currency in which sales prices are denominated/settled)</span>
              <span>The currency of the country whose competitive forces/regulations mainly determine sales prices</span>
              <span>The currency that mainly influences labour, material, and other costs of providing goods/services</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SECONDARY INDICATORS</div>
            <div class="check-list">
              <span>The currency in which funds from financing activities are generated</span>
              <span>The currency in which receipts from operating activities are usually retained</span>
              <span>For a foreign operation: whether its activities are an EXTENSION of the parent's (same functional currency), or carried out with significant AUTONOMY (own functional currency)</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-top:8px;margin-bottom:0;">⚠️ Functional currency, ONCE DETERMINED, is only changed if there is a change in the underlying transactions, events, and conditions that are relevant to it — it cannot be switched simply because management prefers a different currency's presentation.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Foreign Currency Transactions — Initial &amp; Subsequent Recording</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Recording rules</div>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <div class="f-label">INITIAL RECOGNITION</div>
          <div class="f-eq">Record at the SPOT exchange rate on the transaction date (or an average rate that approximates it, if rates don't fluctuate significantly)</div>
        </div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">At EACH subsequent reporting date, translate: monetary items at the CLOSING rate; non-monetary items at historical cost using the HISTORICAL rate; non-monetary items at fair value using the rate WHEN fair value was determined.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Worked example — foreign currency purchase and settlement</div>
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 1: Trade payable denominated in a foreign currency</div>
          <div class="wk-body">
            A UK entity (functional currency GBP) purchases inventory from a US supplier for $120,000 on 1 November (rate: $1.30/£1, so £92,308). Year end is 31 December (rate: $1.25/£1). The invoice is settled on 20 January (rate: $1.20/£1).
          </div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            <strong>Initial recognition (1 Nov)</strong>: 120,000 ÷ 1.30 = £92,308<br>
            <strong>Year-end retranslation (31 Dec)</strong>: 120,000 ÷ 1.25 = £96,000 → <strong style="color:var(--color-text-danger);">FX loss of £3,692</strong> recognised in SOPL (the GBP cost of settling the liability has INCREASED)<br>
            <strong>Settlement (20 Jan)</strong>: 120,000 ÷ 1.20 = £100,000 → a FURTHER <strong style="color:var(--color-text-danger);">FX loss of £4,000</strong> recognised in the following period's SOPL
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">FX gains/losses on monetary items are recognised in SOPL in the period they arise — split across periods if settlement occurs after a reporting date, as shown here.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Monetary vs Non-Monetary Items — The Critical Distinction</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Classification examples</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;"><span>Monetary items</span><span>Non-monetary items</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;"><span>Cash and cash equivalents</span><span>Inventory</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;"><span>Trade receivables/payables</span><span>Property, plant and equipment</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;"><span>Loans and borrowings</span><span>Intangible assets</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;"><span>Provisions to be settled in cash</span><span>Investments in equity instruments (unless held for trading at FVTPL, then treated per its FV measurement date)</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">The test: is the item a right to receive, or obligation to deliver, a FIXED or DETERMINABLE number of units of currency? If yes → monetary. If the amount could vary based on non-currency factors (e.g. physical goods, ownership stakes) → non-monetary.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Worked example — non-monetary asset held at historical cost</div>
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 2: Foreign currency PPE purchase, no retranslation</div>
          <div class="wk-body">
            The same UK entity purchases machinery from Germany for €50,000 on 1 March (rate: €1.15/£1, so £43,478), held under the cost model. At year end (31 Dec), the EUR/GBP rate has moved to €1.10/£1.
          </div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            <strong>Carrying amount at year end</strong> = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">£43,478</strong> (UNCHANGED) — since the machinery is a non-monetary asset held at historical cost, it is NOT retranslated at the closing rate; only its ORIGINAL historical rate applies (subject to normal depreciation).
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout danger" style="margin-bottom:0;">✕ <strong style="font-weight:600">A very common exam trap:</strong> students often incorrectly retranslate non-monetary assets held at cost using the closing rate — only MONETARY items and non-monetary items measured at FAIR VALUE are retranslated at each reporting date.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Translating Into the Presentation Currency</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">The consolidation translation method — with worked example</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1.5fr;"><span>Statement item</span><span>Rate used</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1.5fr;"><span>Assets and liabilities (SOFP)</span><span>CLOSING rate at the end of the reporting period</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1.5fr;"><span>Income and expenses (SOPL)</span><span>Exchange rates AT THE DATES of the transactions (an AVERAGE rate for the period is a permitted approximation, unless rates fluctuated significantly)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1.5fr;"><span>Resulting exchange differences</span><span>Recognised in OCI, accumulated in a separate component of equity (the "foreign currency translation reserve")</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-top:8px;margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 3: Translating a foreign subsidiary's SOFP</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A US parent (functional/presentation currency USD) has a UK subsidiary with net assets of £2,000,000 at year end. Closing rate: $1.28/£1. Opening net assets were £1,800,000, translated at the opening rate of $1.25/£1 = $2,250,000. Profit for the year, translated at the average rate, was $180,000.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            <strong>Closing net assets translated</strong> = 2,000,000 × 1.28 = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">$2,560,000</strong><br>
            <strong>Expected closing net assets</strong> = Opening ($2,250,000) + Profit translated ($180,000) = $2,430,000<br>
            <strong>Exchange difference (gain)</strong> = 2,560,000 − 2,430,000 = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-success);">$130,000 → OCI</strong>, accumulated in the translation reserve
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Disposal of a Foreign Operation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Recycling the translation reserve on disposal</div>
        <div class="principle">On DISPOSAL of a foreign operation, the CUMULATIVE amount of exchange differences relating to that foreign operation, previously recognised in OCI and accumulated in the separate component of equity, is RECLASSIFIED ("recycled") from equity to profit or loss as a reclassification adjustment.</div>
        <div class="callout info" style="margin-bottom:0;">ℹ️ <strong style="font-weight:600">On PARTIAL disposal</strong> (while retaining control, e.g. selling some shares in a subsidiary that remains a subsidiary), only a PROPORTIONATE share of the cumulative amount is reattributed to non-controlling interests — NOT recycled to profit or loss, since control is retained. Only a disposal resulting in loss of control (or significant influence/joint control) triggers full recycling of the parent's share.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Net Investment in a Foreign Operation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Monetary items forming part of the net investment</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;margin-bottom:9px;">Exchange differences on a MONETARY item that forms part of a reporting entity's net investment in a foreign operation (e.g. an intercompany loan not expected to be settled in the foreseeable future, effectively permanent financing) are recognised in OCI in the CONSOLIDATED financial statements, and reclassified to SOPL only on disposal of the foreign operation — even though such a loan would normally be an ordinary monetary item recognised through SOPL.</div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-bottom:0;">⚠️ In the SEPARATE (unconsolidated) financial statements of either the parent or the foreign operation, exchange differences on such a loan are recognised in PROFIT OR LOSS as normal — the special OCI treatment applies ONLY at the consolidated level, since it is only there that the loan is genuinely "quasi-equity" (eliminating intragroup effects).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Required disclosures</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">GENERAL DISCLOSURES</div>
            <div class="check-list">
              <span>The amount of exchange differences recognised in profit or loss (excluding those on financial instruments at FVTPL)</span>
              <span>Net exchange differences accumulated in the separate translation reserve component of equity, and a reconciliation of that amount at the beginning and end of the period</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">FUNCTIONAL/PRESENTATION CURRENCY DISCLOSURES</div>
            <div class="check-list">
              <span>When the presentation currency differs from the functional currency, that fact, together with disclosure of the functional currency and reason for using a different presentation currency</span>
              <span>Any change in functional currency, and the reason for the change</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Determining functional currency, especially for entities with genuinely mixed currency exposure (a fact-based, sometimes finely balanced judgement)</span>
              <span>Whether a foreign operation's activities are carried out with significant autonomy or as an extension of the parent's operations</span>
              <span>Classifying an intercompany loan as forming "part of the net investment" (quasi-equity) versus an ordinary monetary receivable/payable</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Whether exchange rates have fluctuated "significantly," determining if an average rate approximation remains appropriate for SOPL translation</span>
              <span>Appropriate spot rate to apply when multiple exchange rates are available and genuinely exchangeable (per the 2023 IAS 21 lack-of-exchangeability amendments)</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-top:8px;margin-bottom:0;">⚠️ Since a 2023 amendment, IAS 21 provides specific guidance on estimating a spot rate when a currency is NOT exchangeable into another currency — a growing practical issue for entities operating in jurisdictions with currency controls.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>IFRS vs US GAAP — Key Differences</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — IAS 21 vs US GAAP (ASC 830)</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IAS 21)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Core translation methodology</span><span>Functional currency approach — SOFP at closing rate, SOPL at transaction/average rate</span><span>Very similar functional currency approach; largely converged in substance</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Recycling on disposal</span><span>Full recycling of cumulative translation adjustment to SOPL on loss of control/significant influence/joint control</span><span>Similar recycling requirement on sale/substantially complete liquidation of a foreign entity — largely converged</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Hyperinflationary economies</span><span>Governed by IAS 29 — restate financial statements for the effects of hyperinflation before translation</span><span>Uses the REPORTING currency of the parent directly as the functional currency for highly inflationary economies (bypassing separate hyperinflation restatement)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Lack of exchangeability guidance</span><span>Specific guidance added in 2023 amendment for estimating spot rates when a currency lacks exchangeability</span><span>No direct equivalent standalone guidance; addressed through general fair value/estimation principles</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IAS 21 and its US GAAP counterpart are substantially converged on the core translation mechanics — the most significant divergence lies in how each framework handles HYPERINFLATIONARY economies, where the underlying philosophies differ (restate-then-translate under IFRS vs use-parent-currency-directly under US GAAP).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping — Where Every FX Event Lands</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Comprehensive event-to-statement mapping table</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event / transaction</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Initial recognition of a foreign currency transaction</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Recorded at the transaction-date spot rate</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Retranslation of monetary items at each reporting date</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>FX gain/loss recognised immediately</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Non-monetary items at historical cost</span><span>—</span><span>NOT retranslated; historical rate preserved</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Non-monetary items at fair value (revalued/FVTPL/FVOCI)</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span>/<span class="badge bo">OCI</span></span><span>Translated at the date fair value was determined; FX effect follows the underlying item's own gain/loss classification</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Translation of a foreign operation's SOFP for consolidation</span><span><span class="badge bo">OCI</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Closing rate used; resulting difference to translation reserve</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Translation of a foreign operation's SOPL for consolidation</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Transaction-date (or average) rate used</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Disposal of a foreign operation (loss of control)</span><span><span class="badge bo">OCI</span> <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Cumulative translation reserve recycled to SOPL as part of gain/loss on disposal</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Exchange differences on a net-investment intercompany loan (consolidated level)</span><span><span class="badge bo">OCI</span></span><span>Recognised in OCI, recycled only on disposal of the foreign operation</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Same loan in separate financial statements</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Ordinary monetary item treatment — no special OCI deferral</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Cash flows in foreign currency</span><span><span class="badge bs">Cash Flow Statement</span></span><span>Translated at transaction-date rates per IAS 7; FX effect on cash held is a separate reconciling item</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Functional currency determination, translation reserve reconciliation</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Mandatory disclosure</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards — Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">How IAS 21 connects across the IFRS body of literature</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 9</span><span>Derivative instruments used to hedge FX risk (forward contracts, currency swaps) are excluded from IAS 21's transaction rules and follow IFRS 9's hedge accounting/measurement rules instead</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 29</span><span>Financial statements of an entity whose functional currency is that of a HYPERINFLATIONARY economy must be restated under IAS 29 BEFORE translation under IAS 21</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 10</span><span>Determines whether a foreign entity is a subsidiary requiring consolidation and translation under IAS 21's foreign operation rules</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 7</span><span>Determines how foreign currency cash flows are translated for the statement of cash flows, and how the FX effect on cash balances is separately reconciled</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 12</span><span>Exchange differences recognised in OCI create temporary differences whose related deferred tax also follows into OCI, per IAS 12's "tax follows the item" principle</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 2</span><span>Foreign exchange differences on inventory purchases invoiced in foreign currency are generally NOT capitalised into inventory cost under IAS 2, and instead follow IAS 21's normal monetary item treatment</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IFRS 3</span><span>Goodwill and fair value adjustments arising on the acquisition of a foreign operation are treated as ASSETS/LIABILITIES of the foreign operation, and translated at the closing rate along with the rest of its net assets</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="three-col">
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Spot exchange rate</div><div class="glossary-def">The exchange rate for immediate delivery, used to record the initial recognition of foreign currency transactions.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Exchange difference</div><div class="glossary-def">The difference resulting from translating a given number of units of one currency into another currency at different exchange rates.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Net investment in a foreign operation</div><div class="glossary-def">The amount of the reporting entity's interest in the net assets of a foreign operation, including any monetary item accounted for as part of that net investment (e.g. long-term intercompany loans not expected to be settled).</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Reclassification adjustment</div><div class="glossary-def">Amounts reclassified to profit or loss in the current period that were previously recognised in OCI — applies to cumulative translation differences on disposal.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Fair value hedge / cash flow hedge</div><div class="glossary-def">Hedge accounting designations under IFRS 9 that may modify the normal IAS 21 treatment of exchange differences on hedged monetary items.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Translation reserve</div><div class="glossary-def">The separate component of equity accumulating cumulative exchange differences arising on translating foreign operations for consolidation.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="legend">
      <div class="ltitle">Financial statement key</div>
      <div class="lrow">
        <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> Statement of financial position (balance sheet)
        <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> Statement of profit or loss (income statement)
        <span class="badge bo">OCI</span> Other comprehensive income
        <span class="badge be">Equity</span> Statement of changes in equity
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
